<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (Looking at the graph, we can conclude that Coca Colas stock always goes up, then drops and then slowly recovers. This patterns shows how the company reacts to both big world events and normal changes in the economy.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (At the beginning the stock was going up. This makes sense because Coca Cola is a strong and very stable company. People will keep buying its product no matter what is happening with the economy so investors see it as a safe choice. Their strong brand and high profit margins also help keep the stock growing over time. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (Then there is a noticeable drop in the stock price. This is most likely because of Covid 19 and other economic issues. During that time, Coca Cola was affected since a lot of its sales come from places like restaurants , stadiums and travel which where all shot down at the time. Besides that, inflation and higher interest rates made the whole stock go down. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (After that drop, the stock starts to go back up again. This happens because things started reopening and demand came back. Coca Cola also adjusted by focusing more on products like Coke Zero Sugar and improving how it runs its business. As a result, investors started feeling more confident about their decisions in Coca Cola again. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 97 0 R
/E (Overall, the graph looks like this because Coca Cola is a low risk, stable company. Its stock doesn't jump up and down a lot like other companies. Instead it moves more slowly and at an equal paste. That's why even after a drop, it is able to recover over time. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
